// Package phonetic defines how words turn into sound-alike keys and which encoder serves which language.
//
// An Encoder appends one or more keys for a word to a caller's slice. Language packages register their encoder at init with Register, and callers resolve one with For, which accepts a language or locale code ("de", "de-DE", "en_GB") and falls back to English. Importing a language package is what makes it available, so a binary carries only the languages it imports.
//
// English (Double Metaphone), German (Kölner Phonetik), French and Spanish have encoders. Italian has none and falls back to English: on its first corpus (internal/core/match/testdata/phonetic/it.yaml) English Double Metaphone makes 56 of 57 decisions. The grown Italian corpus (internal/core/match/testdata/grown/it.yaml, 364 cases written blind) was meant to settle it, and it did not leave a gap for an encoder to close: English Double Metaphone made 332 of 364 decisions (91.2%, Wilson 95% interval 87.9 to 93.7), and decides all 96 cases in the classes an Italian encoder would model (geminates, c and g before e and i, gn and gli, z and s, silent h, dropped accents). Of the 32 misses, 13 were number forms the normaliser did not parse (compound number words such as "millenovecentonovantadue" and "centoventisei", "otto e mezzo" for 8½); it now reads numbers written as one word and ½, which decided 11 more, for 343 of 364. The rest of the misses are real-word substitutions, splits, letter names, a number joined to a name (Franco126) and absent titles, which no spelling-to-sound rule reaches. No published Italian phonetic algorithm was found to adopt either: the Soundex and Metaphone families are built on English spelling, and Italian adaptations are ad hoc rule sets. An Italian encoder was therefore not built; revisit it if a corpus shows Italian misses that sound-alike keys would fix.
//
// German keeps Kölner Phonetik, and this package's encoder keeps the library's codes. On the grown German corpus (internal/core/match/testdata/grown/de.yaml) English Double Metaphone decided 12 more cases right (+17 / −5 against Kölner Phonetik), and 8 of its gains were a final "-er" heard as "-a" ("lieda" for Lieder, "es war somma"), which Kölner Phonetik keys apart (the r codes 7) while the English encoder also keys a word ending in a vowel and r without its R. The matcher therefore keys a German word of four or more letters ending in "-er" without its r (internal/core/match germanKeys), rather than this encoder doing so. That decides all 8 (527 to 535 of 580) and leaves English 4 ahead (+9 / −5, a difference of −0.7 points, 95% interval −2.0 to +0.6, so no longer distinguishable), with no wrong plays added on either corpus and the same 102 wrong plays per 2,000 absent titles. Keeping the encoder's key and adding the r-less one as a second, as the English encoder does, decides the same cases but makes 103. Nothing in the corpora supports treating "-ern" as "-an". The remaining English gains are English loanwords and names ("skuter" for Scooter, "kraftklup") and c for k ("fitzkaraldo"), which a German rule should not chase.
//
// Every encoder follows one contract: the input is already folded (lowercase, diacritics stripped as match.Normalize does); a word made only of ASCII digits is its own key; a word that encodes to nothing is its own key, so a non-empty word never gets an empty key; there are at most three keys, primary first, without duplicates; keys other than those fallbacks are ASCII; and the encoder is stateless and safe for concurrent use.
package phonetic
